{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>You shall not take vengeance, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim here; Yoma 23a) said: נְקִימָה (taking vengeance) is when one does to him the same evil deed he did; נְטִירָה (bearing a grudge) is when one does not say to him, ״I will not do to you as you did to me.״ According to this, Scripture gave a reason for this commandment by juxtaposing it with ״וְאָהַבְתָּ לְרֵעֲךָ כָּמוֹךָ״ — meaning, that HaShem desires that you love your fellow as yourself, and if you say such a thing to him, you have revealed your intention that hidden in your heart was his improper deed toward you. And He gave a reason for the matter with ״אֲנִי ה׳״ — meaning, in the manner of their statement (Zohar III:16a) that through the unification of hearts His name, blessed be He, is unified, since Knesset Yisrael are the branches of the name of YHWH, blessed be He, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9) ״כִּי חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״." "And HaShem was wise to command in the matter of the conduct of Israel one with another in gradual progression. First commandment: You shall not hate. Second: You shall not take vengeance, etc. Third: And you shall love. And He was precise to say, ״your brother,״ ״your people,״ to say that the commandment applies only to people who act as acts of your people, but the enemies of HaShem, such as apostates and heretics, it is forbidden to love them, and on the contrary one must hate them, as it says (Psalms 139:21) ״הֲלֹא מְשַׂנְאֶיךָ ה׳ אֶשְׂנָא״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:15.572998025Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}